Phase 14 — Migration 0016 fix verification
Date: 2026-09-22
Environment: local (no Docker in this sandbox) — real PostgreSQL 16 +
pgvector 0.6.0, real Redis 7, real Python venv with the project's exact
pinned requirements.txt (fastapi 0.115.0, sqlalchemy 2.0.35, alembic
1.13.2, psycopg[binary] 3.2.2, pgvector 0.3.4).

BUG 1 (root cause): migrations/versions/0016_phase13_analytics_access.py
assumed `adapter_scope` was a native Postgres ENUM type and tried
`ALTER TYPE adapter_scope ADD VALUE`. No such type has ever existed —
installation_credentials.scopes is `ARRAY(String(64))` (see migration
0010, app/models/installation.py), validated at the application layer
by AdapterScope(str, enum.Enum) in app/models/enums.py, which already
defines TELEMETRY_WRITE/TELEMETRY_READ. Fix: removed the ALTER TYPE
calls entirely (no DDL needed) and simplified the downgrade-time scope
cleanup to a plain array UPDATE with no type cast.

BUG 2 (found while fixing bug 1): after removing the ALTER TYPE calls,
upgrade() still explicitly pre-created all 8 new Phase 13 enum types via
`enum_type.create(bind, checkfirst=True)`, then create_table() for
ai_agents / access_policies / page_views / search_demand_events (which
reuse the SAME enum objects, e.g. ACTOR_TYPE in four different tables)
tried to auto-create each type a second time, unconditionally, failing
with "type already exists" — reproduced from a genuinely fresh database
(DROP DATABASE + CREATE DATABASE immediately before), ruling out
leftover state. create_type=False on the Enum objects did not fix this.
Fix: removed the redundant explicit pre-creation loop; create_table()'s
own built-in checkfirst-based type creation correctly creates each
shared type once and reuses it on every later table.

