WORKER VERIFICATION (Section 19/99):

$ celery -A app.workers.celery_app worker --pool=solo  -> "celery@vm
  ready.", connected to redis://localhost:6379/0, all 4 tasks registered
  (documents.ingest, documents.reclaim_stalled, research.execute_task,
  visibility.execute_run)

REAL END-TO-END CHAIN (POST /documents/ingest-url):
  API creates job -> returns job_id, status=queued
  -> worker receives task (confirmed in worker log: "Task documents.ingest[...] received")
  -> BUG 4 found: run_ingestion_job() unconditionally read
     payload["document_id"], but url-kind jobs (created by
     url_ingestion_service.create_url_ingestion_job) never have a
     document_id at creation time -- the whole point of URL ingestion is
     that fetching the URL is what CREATES the document. Every url-kind
     job failed immediately with KeyError('document_id'), was retried
     twice per the task's own retry policy (confirmed: attempts 1->2->3
     in the job status, "retry: Retry in 5s" in the worker log), then
     failed permanently. url_ingestion_service.fetch_and_ingest_url
     already existed, fully implemented and correct, just never called.
  -> Fixed: added a payload.get("kind") == "url" branch in
     run_ingestion_job dispatching to fetch_and_ingest_url, and
     preserved the code's own documented intent that a UrlRejected
     (SSRF-guard rejection) should fail permanently rather than retry
     (retryable=False) -- verified: re-ran the same chain after the fix,
     job now fails in exactly 1 attempt with a URL-fetch error
     (ConnectError) instead of 3 attempts with KeyError.
  -> Progress genuinely persisted to Postgres at every state transition
     and correctly read back via GET /documents/ingestion-jobs/{id} on
     every poll.

REMAINING, NOT AN APPLICATION BUG: the worker's own outbound network
egress is restricted in this sandbox (no general internet access), so
fetch_and_ingest_url's actual HTTP fetch cannot succeed here regardless
of the code being correct -- confirmed the SSRF-guard/fetch layer is
being reached and executing (ConnectError, not KeyError or an import
failure), which is as far as this can be verified without unrestricted
network egress in the worker's environment. This is an environment
constraint (Section 73's NOT_CONFIGURED category), not a code defect.

STATUS: Worker boots, connects to the real broker, receives and
dispatches real tasks, its retry policy behaves exactly as configured,
and job state is durably persisted and correctly readable through the
API at every step. Two real bugs (dispatch + retry classification)
found and fixed by actually running the chain, not by reading the code.

